"Plots each ordered observation against an earlier observation to reveal serial dependence and unusual sequence structure.\n\nUse when: Measurements have a valid acquisition order and a chosen lag. Nonlinear dependence or alternating patterns may be hidden by one correlation coefficient.\n\nAvoid when: The observations are unordered or time gaps make one index lag inconsistent.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes the earlier observation at a declared lag. Vertical position encodes the corresponding later observation.\n\nBuild: Choose a lag with meaningful time or sequence interpretation. Form valid ordered pairs without crossing known sequence breaks. Draw paired points using comparable axes and label the lag in the title.\n\nCheck: Check pairs come from the same sequence and correct lag distance. Verify missing records do not silently compress elapsed time. Compare additional lags or residuals when trend could dominate the pattern.\n\nPitfalls: A patterned cloud can reflect trend or seasonality rather than direct causal dependence. Do not connect points by arbitrary x order; use a connected scatterplot only if temporal path tracing is intended.\n\nExample: Plot synthetic machine vibration at time t against time t−1 to inspect alternating or persistent measurement patterns.\n\nAlternatives: Autocorrelation plot, Residual sequence plot, Connected scatterplot."
